    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4941?c=belmont": {
      "title": "HUB - Graduate Assistant",
      "description": "HUB - Graduate Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) HUB - Graduate Assistant United States | req4941 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/11/2026 Back to Search HUB - Graduate Assistant Employment Type Graduate Assistant Hourly Rate $23.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Responsibilities Collaborate with the Office of Hope, Unity, and Belonging team to plan and execute programs and events that enhance cultural awareness and foster a sense of belonging for all students. Assist in the development and facilitation of belonging training sessions for students, faculty, and staff. Support the coordination of student organizations and affinity groups that promote hope, unity, and a sense of belonging. Conduct research and contribute to creating resources, guides, and materials on topics related to hope, unity, and belonging. Assist with assessing and evaluating HUB initiatives, including data collection and analysis to measure impact and inform future programming. Collaborate with campus departments and external organizations to establish partnerships that promote unity and belonging. Contribute to the development and maintenance of the Office of Hope, Unity, and Belonging's online presence, including social media platforms and website content. Required Qualifications Qualifications Enrolled in a graduate program at Belmont University, preferably in a field related to hope, unity, and belonging, communication, higher education, counseling, social work, or a related discipline. Strong commitment to promoting hope, unity, and belonging on campus. Excellent interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills. Demonstrated ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team. Knowledge and understanding of belonging trends. Experience in event planning, program development, or student leadership is desirable. Proficiency in Microsoft Office and social media platforms. Required Education Enrolled in a graduate program at Belmont University, preferably in a field related to hope, unity, and belonging, communication, higher education, counseling, social work, or a related discipline. Required Experience Experience in event planning, program development, or student leadership is desirable. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more