    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4584?c=belmont": {
      "title": "YMCA/Y-CAP - Program Supporter",
      "description": "YMCA/Y-CAP - Program Supporter | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) YMCA/Y-CAP - Program Supporter United States | req4584 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/14/2026 Back to Search YMCA/Y-CAP - Program Supporter Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $15.00 per hour Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Consistent\nwith the Christian mission and vision of the YMCA of Middle Tennessee, and in\ncooperation with other YMCA staff, the Y-CAP (YMCA Community Action Programs) Program\nVolunteer will help provide kids with positive experiences that enrich their\nlives.  Opportunities to volunteer\ninclude serving as a reading tutor through our literacy program for students\ngrades K-5 (Y-Literacy), mentorship through our after-school social &\nemotional learning program for middle schoolers (Y-Quest), and/or serving as a\nguest speaker or club site volunteer with our college and career readiness\nprogram for high schoolers (Achievers). Program volunteer locations are\navailable in Davidson, Robertson, and Williamson counties. Duties\nand Responsibilities: Complete required training facilitated by program staff. Training prepares volunteers by providing information about the program, expectations, curriculum (where applicable), relationship building, understanding risk factors, mental health issues, how to handle safety issues, and group dynamics. Commit to volunteering for the entire Spring, Summer, or Fall semester and the weekly commitment agreed upon with staff. Most volunteer opportunities require 1 hour per week. Check ins with program staff. Model the YMCA core values: Caring, Honesty, Respect and Responsibility. Understand and support the volunteer programs, goals, and objectives of the YMCA as a community organization. Provide guidance and instruction to youth, teens, and/or adults in assigned program area. Follow and enforce safety regulations and applicable policies, and report any incidents or injuries to YMCA staff. Assist with teaching character development values of caring, honesty, respect, and responsibility. Come dressed for the activities assigned (i.e., tennis shoes for sports activities) Ensure that communication with members, staff, and the public is friendly and professional. Arrive on time for volunteer assignment and be flexible if needs change for volunteer assignment. Carry out all responsibilities in an honest, ethical, and appropriate manner. Perform additional enumerated responsibilities of specific volunteer role. Required Qualifications This position requires and individual with an enthusiastic personality who can develop harmonious relationships with staff, program participants, and other volunteers. Volunteers at the Y make a personal commitment to this vision by welcoming people of all backgrounds and dimensions of diversity. Minimum 18 years of age or older. Satisfactory Background Check and References. Child Abuse Prevention Training. May have to lift up to 30 pounds Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Required Experience Youth Development experience is preferred. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
