    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4897?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Enrollment Services - Student Assistant, Enrollment Marketing & Communications",
      "description": "Enrollment Services - Student Assistant, Enrollment Marketing & Communications | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Enrollment Services - Student Assistant, Enrollment Marketing & Communications United States | req4897 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/12/2026 Back to Search Enrollment Services - Student Assistant, Enrollment Marketing & Communications Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $15 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities The Office of Undergraduate Admissions is hiring student assistants to support the enrollment marketing and communications team. In this role, you will help produce and deliver the print, digital, and social content that reaches prospective students and their families throughout the admissions process, from a student's first inquiry through their decision to enroll at Belmont. This is a hands-on role with real deliverables. You will work alongside marketing and communications staff on live campaigns, contribute to social content that reaches a public audience, and gain practical experience with tools used across the marketing industry, including CRM platforms, email marketing systems, and social media management tools. Key Responsibilities Print & Mail Production Print, assemble, and quality-check admissions materials including letters, viewbooks, brochures, and event collateral Prepare and process mailings, including addressing, stuffing, sorting, and coordinating with campus mail services Maintain inventory of print materials and flag reorder needs Package and ship materials for college fairs, travel season, and off-campus events Data Management Enter, update, and audit prospective student records in the admissions CRM Process inquiry cards, event registrations, and college fair lead lists Assist with list building, data cleanup, and duplicate resolution Social Media & Content Assist with content creation for admissions social channels, including short-form video, photo, graphics, and copy Capture photo and video content at campus visit events, information sessions, and around campus Draft caption copy and support content calendar scheduling Monitor engagement and compile performance summaries Contribute ideas from a current-student perspective on what resonates with prospective students Special Projects & General Support Support campus visit events, preview days, and admitted student programs as needed Assist with web page updates, proofreading, and content formatting Research and organize information for new campaigns and initiatives Other marketing and communications projects as assigned Required Qualifications Required Currently enrolled Belmont University student in good academic standing Strong written communication skills and attention to detail Reliability, discretion, and professionalism, this role involves confidential student information Ability to manage multiple tasks and meet deadlines with limited supervision Comfort with Microsoft Office Preferred Coursework or major in marketing, social media management, public relations, corporate communication, media studies, or a related field Experience creating content for social platforms (Instagram, TikTok, YouTube) Familiarity with Canva, Adobe Creative Cloud, or similar design and video editing tools Basic photography or videography skills Prior customer service, office, or campus involvement experience Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Coursework or major in marketing, social media management, public relations, corporate communication, media studies, or a related field. Required Experience \"no experience required.\" Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4888?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Belmont Mansion - Pianist",
      "description": "Belmont Mansion - Pianist | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Belmont Mansion - Pianist United States | req4888 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/12/2026 Back to Search Belmont Mansion - Pianist Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $15.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Belmont Mansion is seeking a part-time, experienced, and accomplished pianist who enjoys history and historical instruments. Would you enjoy performing in a unique historical setting? Are you intrigued by the opportunity to play a 158-year-old piano in an 1860s era room? Do you wish to strengthen your public speaking skills? If so, we would love to talk with you. An 1860s box grand piano was restored recently with the intent of allowing our visitors to experience mid-19th century music in the authentic period Grand Salon of Belmont Mansion. Forty-five-minute to one-hour performances would take place during regular visitor hours to the museum both during the week and weekends. (Mon - Sat: 10:00 am to 4:00 pm; Sunday: 11:00 to 4:00). Ideal applicants will demonstrate a mastery of the piano and the ability to sight read music, the ability to learn and adapt to playing a historical instrument, the skill set to convey to the public the history of the instrument as well as the music being played (script provide by Belmont Mansion), a willingness to work as one part of a bigger team, a strong public speaking presence, and demonstrate superior customer service skills. Required Qualifications Applicants must be able to climb stairs and have the ability to remain positive, respectful, and flexible in a fast-paced environment. Must have demonstrated mastery of the piano and the ability to sight read music, the ability to learn and adapt to playing a historical instrument, the skill set to convey to the public the history of the instrument as well as the music being played (script provided by Belmont Mansion staff), a willingness to work as one part of a bigger team, a strong public speaking presence, and demonstrate superior customer service skills. Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Must have training and performance experience as a pianist. Required Experience Must have demonstrated mastery of the piano and the ability to sight read music, the ability to learn and adapt to playing a historical instrument, the skill set to convey to the public the history of the instrument as well as the music being played (script provided by Belmont Mansion staff), a willingness to work as one part of a bigger team, a strong public speaking presence, and demonstrate superior customer service skills. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4572?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Belmont Formation Collaborative - Formation Apprentice",
      "description": "Belmont Formation Collaborative - Formation Apprentice | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Belmont Formation Collaborative - Formation Apprentice United States | req4572 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/12/2026 Back to Search Belmont Formation Collaborative - Formation Apprentice Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate 15 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities The Formation Apprenticeship is a meaningful on-campus role for students who want to grow as leaders while helping others do the same. As part of the Belmont Formation Collaborative (BFC), Formation Apprentices support programs and initiatives that strengthen character, leadership, and purpose across campus. Through five areas of emphasis—operations, communications, research, professional development, and peer facilitation—apprentices gain real-world experience, build professional skills, and make a tangible impact on campus culture. Whether helping run events, sharing research, or leading character workshops, apprentices play a key role in shaping a community where students can flourish, living with integrity, purpose, and courage. The BFC consists of nine interdisciplinary units whose work seeks to promote an enduring culture of whole-person formation in every element of the Belmont experience. These units include the BELL Core (General Education), Community-Engaged Learning & Service (CELS), Employee Leadership Development (ELD), Formation Initiatives, Formation Research, Health & Wellness, Interprofessional Education, Spiritual Formation, and the Teaching Center. Apprentices may work more closely with one unit based on strengths and interests. Regardless of unit assignment, all apprentices will engage in the following domains: Operations: Serve as generalists for all BFC units performing routine, entry-level tasks (e.g., WELL Core credit processing, Service Wellness validation). Help plan, execute, and follow up on unit-specific events, workshops, and meetings including setup, teardown, hospitality, and material distribution. Maintain shared spaces, supplies, and equipment used by the BFC team. Character Strength Facilitation: Complete curriculum in character strength interventions and lead workshops for peers in one-on-one, group, classroom, or student organization settings. Professional Development: Participate in team meetings, trainings, and reflection activities to build skills, track growth, and connect your personal development to the mission of the BFC. Communications: Help manage the formation@belmont.edu inbox and promote programs through BFC newsletter, campus event boards, digital signage, and social media. Research: Assist with program assessment and conduct research on best practices in character formation, leadership, and student flourishing. Present key findings in accessible formats like slide decks, summaries, or internal reports. Required Qualifications 1. Critical Thinking & Problem-Solving: - Use logical reasoning and creativity to analyze data and identify meaningful trends. - Evaluate complex information to develop practical solutions that support the goals of character and leadership development programs. 2. Effective Communication: - Articulate ideas clearly and professionally in both written and verbal formats, ensuring that information is shared in a way that is easily understood by diverse audiences. - Adapt communication style and content based on the audience, purpose, and context of interactions. 3. Team Collaboration & Professionalism: - Work effectively with team members, faculty, and staff, demonstrating respect and appreciation for differing perspectives and contributions. - Uphold a high standard of professionalism by being reliable, responsive, and committed to the team’s success. 4. Technology Proficiency: - Confidently use Microsoft Office tools (Word, Excel, PowerPoint) to manage information, create polished reports, and visually communicate findings. - Use Canva to create materials for the BFC team. - Open to learning new technologies and digital tools to support research and project needs. Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Required Experience Interest in character formation, leadership development, or student flourishing\nExperience supporting programs, events, or workshops (e.g., setup, promotion, logistics)\nInvolvement in student organizations, peer leadership, or mentorship roles\nStrong written and verbal communication skills, especially for email, presentations, or social media\nComfort speaking in front of others or a willingness to grow in group facilitation and public speaking\nFamiliarity with tools like Microsoft Office, Canva, or other platforms for communication, organization, or research\nSome experience with data collection (e.g., surveys, interviews, focus groups) is helpful but not required Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4558?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Strategic Venues and Event Services- Production Crew",
      "description": "Strategic Venues and Event Services- Production Crew | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Strategic Venues and Event Services- Production Crew United States | req4558 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/12/2026 Back to Search Strategic Venues and Event Services- Production Crew Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $15.00/hr Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Primary Duties and Responsibilities: As an OSVES Production Crew member, you will\nplay a vital role in ensuring the smooth operation of live concerts and events.\nThis position operates out of the Fisher Center for the Performing Arts, the\nCurb Event Center, and auxiliary event spaces. You will assist with various\nproduction tasks and operation in the respective areas of audio, video,\nlighting, and stage support. This position requires strong communication\nskills, technical proficiency, and the ability to adapt quickly to dynamic\nenvironments. • Assist event staff in audio, video, lighting, and stage\nsupport for live concerts and events. • Operate, organize, and maintain\nproduction equipment to the highest standard. • Communicate effectively with\ncrew, cast, and production team members. • Quickly resolve unexpected issues\nthat arise during shows/events. • Adapt to changing plans and troubleshoot show\nneeds. • Set up and break down equipment before and after events, ensuring all\ngear is properly stored and maintained. • Take initiative and show a\nwillingness to go above and beyond basic responsibilities. Required Qualifications • Strong interest in live event production and performing\narts. • Basic knowledge of audio, video, lighting, and/or stage equipment. •\nExcellent communication and teamwork skills. • Ability to work under pressure\nand solve problems quickly. • Previous experience in a similar role is a plus,\nbut not required. Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Preference to students in related fields (music business - production, audio engineering technology, video production, creative entertainment industry, digital musicianship, etc.) Required Experience No previous employment history is required; however, applicants without job history must clearly give examples of their ability with production skill sets and event operations skill sets in other environments Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
