    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/2397?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Athletics -  Office Assistant",
      "description": "Athletics -  Office Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Athletics -  Office Assistant United States | req2397 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/6/2026 Back to Search Athletics -  Office Assistant Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $13.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Office Assistant will perform basic desk attendant responsibilities and will assist the Assistant Director of Administration. They will work with athletic department employees on various levels. Required Qualifications Some knowledge of Athletics Strong communication, organizational, and customer service skills Experience using Microsoft office and Google Docs Ability to multi-task and work well in a fast-paced, team environment Responsible, dependable, with basic office skills Assist office manager in daily functions Assist all other staff and coaches with various tasks and office work Answer the phone at the front desk and direct all calls Greet and welcome any guests that come into the athletic department Assist student athletes and help mangage the academic center Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Required Experience Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4890?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Law",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Law | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate Professor of Law United States | req4890 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/5/2026 Back to Search Assistant/Associate Professor of Law We are now accepting applications for a qualified Assistant/Associate Professor of Law at Belmont University. Employment Type Faculty Internal Job Category Tenure Track (Faculty) Full-Time Department Law Hours N/A Primary Duties and Responsibilities: The Belmont University College of Law invites applications for multiple tenure-track or tenured faculty positions to begin in Fall 2027. Belmont is open to candidates with research and teaching specialization in the areas of health law, entertainment law, criminal law, criminal procedure, contracts, business associations, and related areas. The Belmont University College of Law encourages applications from people whose background, life experiences, and scholarly approaches would contribute to the diversity of our faculty, curriculum, and programs. Applicants must possess a J.D. or equivalent degree from an accredited U.S. law school and must demonstrate strong scholarly potential and a commitment to excellence in teaching. Belmont is an EOE/AA employer.  Belmont College of Law reserves the right to exercise a preference for those candidates who support the goals and missions of the University. If you have questions about the position or Belmont University, please contact, Chair of the Faculty Recruitment Committee,  Professor Gluzman at maximiliano.gluzman@belmont.edu. Belmont University is a private, Christian university focusing on academic excellence. It is located in the heart of Nashville, one of the fastest growing and most culturally rich cities in the country.  Belmont is the second largest private university in Tennessee with approximately 9,000 students. Belmont students come from every state, more than 35 countries, and all faiths. The Belmont Law faculty is dedicated to high quality teaching, service to the school and the profession, and active engagement in scholarship. Ranked 85th among ABA-Accredited law schools by U.S. News and World Report, Belmont Law’s median LSAT and GPA for the 136 students who entered the law school in August 2025 were 161 and 3.84 (75th percentile: 163 and 3.94; 25th percentile: 158 and 3.66).  Belmont Law had the highest bar passage rate in the State of Tennessee in July of 2025 (97%), and a 100% first-time bar passage rate in thirteen other jurisdictions. Belmont Law’s practice-ready pedagogy is supported by collaborative faculty members excited to bring their practical legal expertise to their scholarship and teaching; by a robust experiential learning program; by an award-winning advocacy program; by unique study abroad experiences; and by certificate programs in health law, criminal law, and entertainment law. Required Qualifications : Belmont University seeks to attract and retain highly qualified faculty and staff who will support our mission and vision. As a Christ-centered and student-focused community, we aim to: Form diverse leaders of character; Equip people to solve the world’s complex problems through teaching, research and service; And be radical champions for helping people and communities flourish. Knowledge of principles, methods and current developments in the field of study. Knowledge of subject matter and course content taught. Knowledge of instructional strategies and techniques. Demonstrated capacity to engage in high quality scholarly activity. Demonstrated ability to advise, instruct, manage, motivate and evaluate students. Skilled in the use of computers, Internet resources, and MS Office software. Ability to learn relevant sections of BannerWeb, Belmont University’s online integrated database system for student grading. Ability to integrate technology into the teaching and learning process. Ability to successfully interact with students, other educators and educational institution representatives, and the general public in a professional manner. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively verbally and in writing. Knowledge of the programs and services offered by Belmont University. Interest and ability to work collaboratively in course design and to teach interdisciplinary and topical courses. Ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies. Required Education J.D. Required. Required Experience Higher education teaching experience preferred. The selected candidate will be required to complete a background check satisfactory to the University. Belmont University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4521?c=belmont": {
      "title": "College of Nursing - Lead Peer Tutor",
      "description": "College of Nursing - Lead Peer Tutor | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) College of Nursing - Lead Peer Tutor United States | req4521 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/5/2026 Back to Search College of Nursing - Lead Peer Tutor Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $13 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Position Overview The Lead Peer Tutor role is designed for nursing students who provide academic support to designated student cohorts based on program progression. Rather than selecting a single course, each Lead Peer Tutor will support a course(s) aligned with a specific level of the nursing curriculum. This role emphasizes collaborative leadership, relationship-building, and the promotion of independent, active learning strategies. Lead Peer Tutors serve as mandatory group tutors, develop academic resources, and offer guidance to fellow peer tutors. They play a key role in helping students build confidence, adopt effective study strategies, and approach their coursework with resilience and a growth mindset. Cohort Assignments The Lead Peer Tutor team will consist of five tutors, each assigned to one of the following groups: Freshman/Prerequisite Sciences Courses: Microbiology, Anatomy & Physiology I (Fall), Anatomy & Physiology II (Spring) Level 2 Course: Foundations of Care Management Course: Pathophysiology Level 3 – Group A Courses: Care 1, Pharmacology 1 Level 3 – Group B Courses: Care 2, Pharmacology 2 *Level 4 (Care III and Care IV) will be supported by alumni tutors and the Student Success Graduate Assistant* Primary Responsibilities Commit to at least one academic year of employment. Work a maximum of 8 hours per week Serve as a lead tutor for a designated cohort of peer tutors, based on program progression Provide group tutoring for courses aligned to each level, based on your interests and experience. Develop and share at least one high-quality review tool or practice resource per semester for the NUR course you support. These will be added to the ICON Peer Tutoring Resource Library. Host optional virtual office hours (time-permitting) to support fellow peer tutors—offering guidance, sharing strategies, and troubleshooting challenges. Communicate promptly with the Student Success Coordinator and provide feedback about student needs or recurring challenges. Participate in a tutor training focused on adult learning principles, inclusive support, and ethical practices. Uphold confidentiality in accordance with FERPA and program policies. Required Qualifications Must be a nursing student enrolled at Belmont University! Earned a minimum grade of “B” or higher in chosen course Strong interpersonal and communication skills High level of motivation and initiative Strong organizational and time management skills Commitment to consistent availability and support for assigned cohort Complete a brief interview with the Student Success team Willingness to serve as a resource guide, and collaborative peer Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Must be an undergraduate nursing student who will be enrolled at Belmont for the entire academic year. Required Experience Tutoring experience a plus. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/1393?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Athletics - Tutor for Student-Athletes",
      "description": "Athletics - Tutor for Student-Athletes | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Athletics - Tutor for Student-Athletes United States | req1393 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 8/5/2026 Back to Search Athletics - Tutor for Student-Athletes Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $15.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Must be recommended by a professor or departmental office\nstaff; ability to teach; flexible schedule; understanding of the\nmaterial/subject matter. In order for me to hire you as a tutor, I need to have\na professor or departmental staff member recommend you to tutor in that\nspecific subject area. Please have a professor send me an email recommendation\nto ab.armbruster@belmont.edu. Tutors are hired in ALL subjects except writing\nbased courses (i.e. English) Required Qualifications Must be recommended by a professor or departmental office staff; ability to teach; flexible schedule; understanding of the material/subject matter. Required Education Must be enrolled as a current student at Belmont University. Required Experience Must have taken the class before and/or majoring in the subject area you'll be tutoring in. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
