    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4863?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, History and Theory of Architecture",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor, History and Theory of Architecture | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant/Associate Professor, History and Theory of Architecture United States | req4863 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/13/2026 Back to Search Assistant/Associate Professor, History and Theory of Architecture We are now accepting applications for a qualified Assistant/Associate Professor, History and Theory of Architecture at Belmont University. Employment Type Faculty Internal Job Category Tenure Track (Faculty) Full-Time Department Architecture Hours N/A Primary Duties and Responsibilities: The O’More College\n  of Architecture and Design at Belmont University in Nashville invites\n  applications for a tenure-track faculty position in the History and Theory of\n  Architecture at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, with an\n  anticipated start date of January or August 2027. The Department of\n  Architecture seeks candidates who are committed to educating diverse leaders\n  of character and who view architecture as a vehicle for the greater good.\n  Grounded in Belmont’s Christ-centered mission, the program emphasizes\n  collaborative, interdisciplinary learning; the integration of creativity,\n  empathy, and technical rigor; and the development of meaningful, human-centered\n  design approaches that engage community, context, stewardship, and\n  professional practice. Responsibilities of\n  the position include teaching 24 credit/workload hours per year in the\n  architecture program, with an emphasis on the history and theory of\n  architecture in one or more of the following areas: A holistic approach to the histories of architecture,\n       including vernacular traditions and professionally-designed buildings in\n       a global context Theories of architecture, especially as they intersect\n       with ecological concerns History of architecture and the built environment in\n       the Southeast Additional\n  responsibilities include collegial participation in collaborative settings,\n  academic advising, student mentoring, scholarly activity, and college and\n  university service.  The successful candidate is expected to pursue\n  these aspects of faculty life within an ecumenical, student-focused\n  environment that brings together the best of liberal arts and professional\n  education in a Christ-centered community of learning and service. Located near the heart of thriving Nashville, Tennessee, Belmont University consists of nearly 9,000 students who come from every state and 33 countries. The University is nationally recognized for its innovative approach as well as its commitment to undergraduate teaching. As a Christ-centered, student-focused community, Belmont’s mission is to develop diverse leaders of character, wisdom and a transformational mindset. With more than 130 areas of undergraduate study, 29 master’s programs and eight doctoral degrees, Belmont University aims to be the leading Christ-centered university in the world, producing leaders who will radically champion the pursuit of life abundant for all people. O’More College\n  of Architecture and Design at Belmont University is a creative\n  community that serves undergraduate students by providing\n  professionally-oriented, liberal arts-based programs in Fashion Design, Fashion Business & Design Leadership, a CIDA-accredited program in Interior Design, and an Architecture\n  program that is accredited by NAAB effective Jan 2025. The facilities of the\n  College include extensive studios that feature industry-standard equipment,\n  flexible classroom/seminar space, a wood/model shop, and a Digital Fabrication\n  Lab. The curricula of all programs benefit from the University’s general\n  education core to develop well-rounded, critical thinkers who can solve\n  problems in diverse groups while focusing on subjects central to their\n  disciplines. In addition to benefiting from this rich liberal arts tradition\n  and curricula focused on professional preparation within a collaborative,\n  interdisciplinary environment dedicated to creative exploration within a\n  Christian environment, students have access to internship opportunities and\n  study abroad programs. Preparing students for their chosen professions, O'More College  programs emphasize the values of sustainability in all its realms, equity,\n  and service. The Department of Architecture offers a five-year B.Arch. that equips\n  graduates with the first formal credential for professional licensure as an\n  architect; the Department also offers a four-year B. F. A. in Architectural\n  Studies. Focused on design, history, theory, structures, and environmental\n  systems within a curriculum linked within the program and to external\n  disciplines, departmental coursework and extracurricular activities stress\n  the values of environmental and cultural stewardship, community service, and\n  professional ethics. Students benefit from integrated coursework that joins\n  their learning across architecture’s sub-fields and throughout the campus. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue\n  until the position is filled. For fullest consideration, please submit all materials\n  by August 31. Questions concerning the position or\n  application materials may be directed to fernando.lima@belmont.edu. For additional information about the position and to complete the online application, candidates are directed to https://jobs.belmont.edu. Required Qualifications : Belmont University seeks to attract and retain highly qualified faculty and staff who will support our mission and vision. As a Christ-centered and student-focused community, we aim to: Form diverse leaders of character; Equip people to solve the world’s complex problems through teaching, research and service; And be radical champions for helping people and communities flourish. Belmont University seeks faculty who will advance its Christ-centered, student-focused mission of preparing diverse leaders of purpose, character, wisdom, and transformational vision. Consistent with the Department of Architecture’s commitment to architecture as a vehicle for the common good, successful candidates will support collaborative and interdisciplinary learning environments that cultivate creativity, empathy, technical excellence, and meaningful, human-centered design approaches that serve communities, steward creation, and inspire hope. Ph.D. in History of Architecture/Art or\n     closely related field with a demonstrated focus on the built environment;\n     ABD candidates will be considered, with degree to be completed during the\n     first year of the appointment Capacity to teach in response to the University and College's\n     mission, vision, and values, integrating faith with teaching and other\n     faculty responsibilities Interest in, and capacity for, teaching additional\n     subject matter in the architecture program, and/or the University’s\n     general education program Teaching experience in higher education, especially in\n     a NAAB-accredited program Preferred\nQualifications: Professional degree in Architecture Professional experience/licensure or being\n     licensure-ready Research/creative agenda that is amenable to student\n     participation During the application process, applicants will be asked to attach a cover letter, Curriculum Vita/Resume and prepare a one-page essay, 300-400 words, describing how your core beliefs and practices inform your support of our Christ-Centered mission and influence your teaching, research, and service philosophy. Required Education Ph.D. in History of Architecture/Art or closely related field with a demonstrated focus on the built environment; ABD candidates will be considered, with degree to be completed during the first year of the appointment Required Experience Teaching experience in higher education The selected candidate will be required to complete a background check satisfactory to the University. Belmont University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4130?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Student Success & Flourishing - Office Assistant",
      "description": "Student Success & Flourishing - Office Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Student Success & Flourishing - Office Assistant United States | req4130 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/7/2026 Back to Search Student Success & Flourishing - Office Assistant Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $10.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Our primary\nresponsibility is to provide support to your fellow Belmont Students as a part\nof a professional team.  Student Success and Flourishing Suite 214 consists Career & Professional\nDevelopment, GPS, International Student Services, and Assistant Provost of\nStudent Success and Flourishing. The\nStudent Office Assistant will support the administrative team by performing a\nvariety of clerical and office tasks. This role is designed to provide students\nwith practical experience in an office environment while balancing their\nacademic commitments. Staffing welcome desk and assist all offices with office operations, including: Serving as first point of contact when students and visitors enter the office for assistance. Answer the phone and direct all calls. Handle\nincoming and outgoing mail and deliveries. Scheduling appointments and providing clerical support as needed. Connect students with other members of the professional staff. Understanding student needs as they present, complete assigned project work. Expectation to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies in a diverse community. Required Qualifications Qualifications for the Student Office Assistant: Must have FWS Award to apply Proficiency\nin Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint) and basic computer skills. Strong\nverbal and written communication abilities. Ability\nto manage time effectively and handle multiple tasks. Reliable\nand punctual with a strong work ethic. Ability\nto work well with others and provide excellent customer service. Maintain\na professional demeanor in the office environment. Ability\nto handle sensitive information appropriately. You may be asked to assist with these areas of support as your skills,\n  experiences and interest show: Opportunity to work with other Belmont administrative and academic departments. Assist with social media and marketing.  Graphic design experience is a plus. Assisting marketing ideas, heartfelt student outreach, printing newsletters, etc. Creating social media campaigns. Assist with data collection and entry. Provide logistical support for events. Helping with miscellaneous office duties as needed. Required Education Must be enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Required Experience no experience required. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/2736?c=belmont": {
      "title": "COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship - House Of Store Associate",
      "description": "COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship - House Of Store Associate | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship - House Of Store Associate United States | req2736 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/7/2026 Back to Search COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship - House Of Store Associate Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate 18.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities House Of is a student-run women's clothing boutique\nlocated on Belmont Blvd adjacent to campus, opened in 2015. The store features\nalumni- and student-designed products and operates as a live retail environment\nwhere students manage real business operations across five functional teams.\nAssociates do not simply staff the store. They run it. Serve as an active member of one of five teams:\nFinance, Merchandise and Inventory, Marketing, Events, or Management. Placement\nis determined after the interview; applicants may indicate a preference. Finance: Track weekly sales performance, manage the store budget, and support\npricing and markdown decisions. Merchandise and Inventory: Make bi-weekly\nbuying decisions using sales data, manage vendor relationships, and maintain\nproduct records in Clover. Marketing: Run the store marketing calendar across\nInstagram, flyers, and campus channels and track which efforts drive traffic. Events: Plan and execute in-store and on-campus events, coordinating logistics,\nvendors, and cross-team promotion. Management: Hold teams accountable, manage\nthe staff schedule, and run Monday store meetings. Attend Monday staff\nmeetings at 10:00 a.m. Attendance is required. Support daily operations\nincluding opening, closing, and in-store setup. Minimum 10 hours per week\nrequired. Required Qualifications Strong organizational and time management skills. Ability to work independently and take ownership of assigned responsibilities\nwith minimal supervision. Interest in fashion, retail, branding, or\nentrepreneurship. Entrepreneurial mindset with a willingness to learn from\nreal business decisions and their outcomes. Effective written and verbal\ncommunication skills. Ability to collaborate across teams and manage\ncompeting priorities. Graphic design or digital marketing skills are a plus. Availability to attend Monday staff meetings at 10:00 a.m. is required. Required Education • Must be currently enrolled as an undergraduate student at Belmont University. \n• Sophomore standing or above required. Required Experience Retail, entrepreneurship, or fashion experience is a plus Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/2445?c=belmont": {
      "title": "COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship – Bruin Bodega Store Associate",
      "description": "COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship – Bruin Bodega Store Associate | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship – Bruin Bodega Store Associate United States | req2445 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/7/2026 Back to Search COBA, Center for Entrepreneurship – Bruin Bodega Store Associate Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $18 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Bruin Bodega is a student-run campus market opened in\nOctober 2025, spotlighting alumni-founded food brands, student consumer\npackaged goods, and products from local Nashville vendors including Mesa Komal\n(Conexion Americas). The Bodega is open to both the Belmont community and\nNashville neighbors. Associates do not simply staff the store. They run it. Serve as an active member of one of five teams: Finance,\nInventory, Marketing, Events, or Management. Placement is determined after the\ninterview; applicants may indicate a preference. Finance: Track weekly sales\nperformance, manage the store budget, and support pricing/markdown decisions. Inventory: Manage weekly buying for perishables and shelf-stable products,\noversee vendor relationships, and track products. Marketing: Run the store\nmarketing calendar across campus channels and track which efforts drive\ntraffic. Events: Plan and execute in-store and on-campus events, coordinating\nlogistics, vendors, and cross-team promotion. Management: Hold teams\naccountable, manage the staff schedule, and run store meetings. Assist with\nfood service duties, including serving samples and operating the in-store scoop\nshop. Attend Monday staff meetings at 10:00 a.m. Attendance is required. Support daily operations (opening, closing, and in-store setup) Minimum 10\nhours per week. Required Qualifications Strong organizational and time management skills. Ability to work independently and take ownership of assigned responsibilities\nwith minimal supervision. Interest in food business, retail,\nentrepreneurship, or consumer packaged goods. Entrepreneurial mindset with a\nwillingness to learn from real business decisions and their outcomes. Effective written and verbal communication skills. Friendly and proactive\napproach to customer interaction. Ability to collaborate across teams and\nmanage competing priorities. Availability to attend Monday staff meetings at\n10:00 a.m. is required. Required Education Must be currently enrolled as a student at Belmont University. Required Experience • No prior experience required. \n• Experience in food service, retail, customer service, or entrepreneurship is preferred. \n• Familiarity with food handling practices is a plus. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/1662?c=belmont": {
      "title": "College of Education - Office Assistant",
      "description": "College of Education - Office Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) College of Education - Office Assistant United States | req1662 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/7/2026 Back to Search College of Education - Office Assistant Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $10.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Are you creative? Do\nyou want to grow your leadership experience? Do you like helping others? If you\nanswered yes to any of these questions, this position might be for you! Duties will include: Assisting the COE Assistant to\n     the Dean with answering telephones, running errands to campus offices, and\n     other office duties. Assisting the COE Admissions\n     Coordinator with marketing ideas, heartfelt student outreach, running\n     campus errands, delivering happies, printing newsletters, etc. Assisting the COE faculty with\n     classroom needs such as preparing copies, typing documents, assembling\n     information packets. Serving as first point of\n     contact when students and visitors enter the office for assistance. Creating social media\n     campaigns Organizing student correspondence\n     and records. Helping with miscellaneous\n     office duties as needed. Required Qualifications Must be efficient in Word and Excel. Must have Federal Work Study to be considered. Must be available to work at least two to three days\nout of the week, for a minimum of 3 hours each day. Desired traits: Engaging Hardworking Innovative Takes Initiative Time Management Creative Required Education Must be enrolled as a current student at Belmont University. Required Experience N/A Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The Un