    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/2407?c=belmont": {
      "title": "FitRec - Facilities Assistant",
      "description": "FitRec - Facilities Assistant | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) FitRec - Facilities Assistant United States | req2407 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/21/2025 Back to Search FitRec - Facilities Assistant Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $10.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Facility Assistants serve as the face of Belmont University's Department of Fitness and Recreation, supporting daily operations across a variety of recreational spaces, including the fitness center, climbing wall, Picklebeach, and Esports Studio. Team members provide exceptional customer service, assist participants, monitor facility spaces, enforce departmental policies, and help maintain safe, clean, and welcoming environments. Responsibilities may vary depending on the assigned program area but all positions contribute to creating exceptional recreational experiences for the Belmont community. Facility Assistants work 6–15 hours per week, including evening and weekend shifts. Required Qualifications Excellent oral communication skills with co-workers, supervisors, patrons and guests Excellent customer interaction skills with co-workers, supervisors, patrons and guests Enforce policies of the Department of Fitness and Recreation regularly, especially amongst peers Ability to work outside conventional work hours (e.g. weekends, late nights and some holidays) Basic knowledge of Microsoft Office suite Required Education Must be enrolled as a current student at Belmont University. Required Experience N/A Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/1853?c=belmont": {
      "title": "FitRec - Intramural Official and Scorekeeper",
      "description": "FitRec - Intramural Official and Scorekeeper | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) FitRec - Intramural Official and Scorekeeper United States | req1853 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/13/2025 Back to Search FitRec - Intramural Official and Scorekeeper Employment Type Student Employee Hourly Rate $10.00 Special Note: All students are required to read the BU Student Employment Policy prior to submitting an application. All Federal Work-Study students should read the program rules and regulations located at http://www.belmont.edu/sfs/employment/fws.html Primary Duties and Responsibilities Intramural Officials and Scorekeepers facilitate a variety of intramural sports and activities by officiating contests, enforcing rules, recording scores and rosters, and creating a fun, safe, and inclusive environment for participants. No previous officiating experience is required, as all staff receive comprehensive training and ongoing development. Duties Include: Officiate intramural sports and activities while enforcing league rules and departmental policies. Record and manage game scores, participant attendance, and team rosters. Foster a safe, welcoming, and inclusive environment that emphasizes sportsmanship and community. Communicate effectively with participants to resolve conflicts and address questions during competition. Attend required trainings, staff meetings, reunions, and professional development opportunities. Assist with the setup, operation, and breakdown of intramural events and competitions. Perform other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications Excellent oral communication skills with co-workers, supervisors, patrons and guests Excellent customer interaction skills with co-workers, supervisors, patrons and guests Ability to work outside conventional work hours (e.g. weekends, late nights) Ability to keep up with potentially rapid pace of play; Ability to effectively enforce rules, especially among peers The physical\nrequirements described here are representative of those that must be met by an\nemployee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job.\nReasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities\nto perform the essential functions. While performing the duties of this job,\nthe employee’s vision must be adequate to read correspondence, computer screen,\nforms, etc. The employee is regularly required to talk and hear. The employee\nfrequently is required to stand; walk; use hands to finger, handle or feel; and\nreach with hands and arms. This position is very active and requires standing,\nwalking, bending, kneeling, stooping, crouching, crawling and climbing all day.\nThe employee must frequently lift and move loads of 20-50 pounds. Close vision,\ndistance vision, peripheral vision, depth perception and the ability to adjust\nfocus, with or without corrective lenses. Required Education Must be enrolled as a current student at Belmont University. Required Experience Prior experience with playing and/or officiating organized sports is preferred, but not required. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4195?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Music Business - Adjunct Instructor (Pool)",
      "description": "Music Business - Adjunct Instructor (Pool) | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Music Business - Adjunct Instructor (Pool) United States | req4195 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 7/7/2025 Back to Search Music Business - Adjunct Instructor (Pool) We are now accepting applications for a qualified Music Business - Adjunct Instructor (Pool) at Belmont University. Employment Type Adjunct (part time) Internal Job Category Adjunct Part-Time Department Music Business Hours Primary Duties and Responsibilities: Belmont University is seeking part-time instructors to teach classes in the music business program, including Music Publishing, Artist Management, Survey of the Music Industry, Copyright Controls, Concert Promotion, Public Relations in the Music Industry, and other music business classes. Assigned up to nine workload hours per semester. Create syllabus, timeline of classes, for students each semester. Provide meeting time for students. Manage online grading system and submit grades in a timely fashion. Abide by all relevant university and/or department procedures and policies. The above statements describe the job’s essential responsibilities and requirements. They are not an exhaustive list of the duties that may be assigned to job incumbents. Required Qualifications : Belmont University seeks to attract and retain highly qualified faculty and staff who will support our mission and vision. As a Christ-centered and student-focused community, we aim to: Form diverse leaders of character; Equip people to solve the world’s complex problems through teaching, research and service; And be radical champions for helping people and communities flourish. Knowledge of principles, methods and current developments in the field of study. Knowledge of subject matter and course content taught. Knowledge of instructional strategies and techniques. Demonstrated ability to support, instruct, manage, motivate and evaluate students. Skilled in the use of computers, Internet resources, and MS Office software. Ability to learn relevant sections of BannerWeb, Belmont University’s online integrated database system for student grading. Ability to integrate technology into the teaching and learning process. Ability to successfully interact with students, other educators and educational institution representatives, and the general public in a professional manner. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively verbally and in writing. Knowledge of the programs and services offered by Belmont University. Ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies. Required Education Bachelor's degree in a related discipline or equivalent experience required. Master's or terminal degree preferred. Required Experience Higher education teaching experience preferred. The selected candidate will be required to complete a background check satisfactory to the University. Belmont University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
