    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4987?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pediatric Physical Therapy",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Pediatric Physical Therapy | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Assistant Professor of Pediatric Physical Therapy United States | req4987 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Assistant Professor of Pediatric Physical Therapy We are now accepting applications for a qualified Assistant Professor of Pediatric Physical Therapy at Belmont University. Employment Type Faculty Internal Job Category Tenure Track (Faculty) Full-Time Department Coll Pharmacy&Health Sciences Hours Primary Duties and Responsibilities: Position purpose: Assistant\nProfessor in Physical Therapy serving as lead faculty member in pediatric\ncontent. Job functions: Complete equivalent of 18 workload hours per academic year. Teach in the entry-level DPT program Advise and mentor students in research and other class-related projects. Engage in activities to support the mission and vision of Belmont University and the college in which you teach. Participate in departmental and university committee work. Engage in scholarly activity and professional development. Participate in a local church and the community. Abide by all relevant university and/or department procedures and policies. Manage online grading system. Timely submission of book orders, if textbooks required. Maintain regular office hours. The above statements describe the job’s essential responsibilities and requirements. They are not an exhaustive list of the duties that may be assigned to job incumbents. Required Qualifications : Belmont University seeks to attract and retain highly qualified faculty and staff who will support our mission and vision. As a Christ-centered and student-focused community, we aim to: Form diverse leaders of character; Equip people to solve the world’s complex problems through teaching, research and service; And be radical champions for helping people and communities flourish. Knowledge and skill in pediatric Physical Therapy Demonstrated capacity to engage in high quality scholarly activity. Demonstrated ability to advise, instruct, manage, motivate and evaluate students. Skilled in the use of computers, Internet resources, and MS Office software. Ability to learn relevant sections of Canvas (learning management system) and BannerWeb, Belmont University’s online integrated database system for student grading. Ability to integrate technology into the teaching and learning process. Ability to successfully interact with students, other educators and educational institution representatives, and the general public in a professional manner. Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively verbally and in writing. Knowledge of the programs and services offered by Belmont University. Interest and ability to work collaboratively in course design and to teach interdisciplinary and topical courses. Ability to work effectively with a wide range of constituencies. Required Education Preferred academic doctorate or DPT with ABPTS Pediatric Specialty Certification (PCS). Eligible for Tennessee Physical Therapy license. Required Experience A minimum of 4 years of clinical experience with pediatric patients and/or teaching experience. The selected candidate will be required to complete a background check satisfactory to the University. Belmont University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4985?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Scientist Educator",
      "description": "Scientist Educator | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Scientist Educator United States | req4985 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Scientist Educator We are now accepting applications for a qualified Scientist Educator at Belmont University. Employment Type Faculty Internal Job Category Tenure Track (Faculty) Full-Time Department College of Medicine Hours N/A Primary Duties and Responsibilities: The Department of Foundational and\nClinical Sciences (FCS) at the Thomas F. Frist, Jr. College of Medicine (FCoM)\nat Belmont University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-eligible Scientist\nEducator faculty position. The successful candidate will join a\ncollaborative, mission-driven faculty committed to developing and delivering an\ninnovative, integrated medical curriculum grounded in whole-person care and\ndedicated to the College’s vision of transforming health through compassion and\nservice. The Scientist Educator will contribute\nto the academic mission of the College of Medicine through teaching,\nscholarship, and service. Candidates should possess expertise in one or more\nfoundational biomedical science disciplines, such as biochemistry,\npathology, physiology, pharmacology, microbiology, immunology, neuroscience,\nanatomy or a related biomedical science field. The successful candidate will provide\nhigh-quality instruction across the pre-clerkship medical curriculum and work\ncollaboratively with foundational science and clinical faculty to integrate\nbasic science concepts with clinical applications. The Scientist Educator\nshould be able to help medical students understand and apply foundational\nscientific principles to mechanisms of disease, diagnosis, treatment,\nprevention, and patient care. The position also includes mentoring\nlearners, engaging in scholarly activity, participating in curriculum\ndevelopment and assessment, and contributing to departmental and College\ninitiatives, including those supporting continuous quality improvement and\naccreditation. Responsibilities: Teaching and\nCurriculum Development Deliver engaging, evidence-based instruction in areas of expertise\n     through lectures, laboratories, small-group sessions, case-based learning,\n     and other instructional formats. Develop, revise, and maintain learning objectives, instructional\n     materials, and assessments aligned with course and program outcomes and\n     accreditation standards. Integrate foundational biomedical science concepts across\n     organ-system and interdisciplinary courses and connect scientific\n     principles with clinical cases and patient care. Collaborate with foundational science and clinical faculty to\n     promote horizontal and vertical integration throughout the medical\n     curriculum. Utilize innovative and evidence-based teaching strategies, including\n     active learning, case-based learning, and technology-enhanced instruction. Participate in the development and continuous improvement of\n     courses, assessments, and curricular content. Mentor and advise medical students in their academic, professional,\n     and scholarly development. Scholarship and\nResearch Maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to the\n     candidate’s area of expertise and academic interests, which may include\n     biomedical, translational, clinical, or medical education research. Disseminate scholarly work through peer-reviewed publications,\n     presentations, educational products, or other appropriate scholarly\n     venues. Pursue internal and external funding opportunities, when\n     appropriate, to support research, educational innovation, or collaborative\n     scholarly initiatives. Mentor and supervise medical students participating in research and\n     scholarly activities. Develop interdisciplinary collaborations within the College of\n     Medicine, Belmont University, and the broader academic and healthcare\n     community. Remain current with advances in biomedical science, medical\n     education, and areas of disciplinary expertise and incorporate relevant\n     developments into teaching and scholarship. Service and\nLeadership Participate in departmental, College, and University committees and contribute to shared governance and strategic initiatives. Contribute to accreditation activities, program evaluation, and continuous quality improvement. Engage in professional service through relevant scientific, educational, or professional organizations. Contribute to curriculum innovation, program development, and other initiatives supporting the educational mission of the College. Support a collegial academic environment characterized by collaboration, professionalism, respect, and commitment to the mission and values of Belmont University and the College of Medicine. Appointment and\nEvaluation This is a full-time, 12-month,\ntenure-eligible faculty appointment in the Department of Foundational and\nClinical Sciences. Faculty rank and salary will be commensurate with\nqualifications and experience. Tenure-track faculty are evaluated on\nperformance in teaching, scholarship, and service consistent with the Belmont\nUniversity Faculty Handbook and College of Medicine criteria for academic\nadvancement. About the Thomas\nF. Frist, Jr. College of Medicine The\nThomas F. Frist, Jr. College of Medicine at Belmont University in Nashville,\nTennessee, is committed to educating physicians who embrace whole-person care,\ncompassionate service, and lifelong learning. Founded on Belmont University’s\nChrist-centered mission, the College seeks faculty who value collaboration,\nacademic excellence, service, innovation, and the formation of physicians\nprepared to care for patients and communities with knowledge, compassion, and\nintegrity. Required Qualifications : Belmont University seeks to attract and retain highly qualified faculty and staff that share the University's values and will contribute to its mission and vision to be a leader among teaching universities bringing together the best of liberal arts and professional education in a Christian community of learning and service. Ability to connect foundational scientific principles with\n     mechanisms of disease and their clinical application. Commitment to innovative, learner-centered pedagogy and\n     interdisciplinary integration of foundational and clinical sciences. Commitment to scholarship and the mentorship of medical students in\n     scholarly activities. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and demonstrated\n     ability to work collaboratively within a multidisciplinary academic\n     environment. Preferred Experience teaching within a medical school or other health\n     professions education program. Experience in an integrated, organ-system-based, or case-based\n     medical curriculum. Ability to contribute meaningfully to instruction across multiple\n     areas of the foundational medical sciences. Record of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed\n     publications, conference presentations, funded research, educational\n     scholarship, or other forms of scholarly activity. Experience mentoring students in biomedical science, translational\n     research, or medical education scholarship. Familiarity with active-learning methodologies, educational\n     technologies, and contemporary approaches to medical education. Experience\nwith curriculum design, assessment, program evaluation, and accreditation\nprocesses, particularly within an LCME-accredited medical education program. Required Education Terminal degree (PhD, MD, DO, or equivalent) in a foundational biomedical science discipline or a closely related field. Required Experience Demonstrated expertise in one or more areas of foundational biomedical science relevant to medical education. Evidence of effective teaching or demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching at the undergraduate, graduate, health professions, or medical school level. The selected candidate will be required to complete a background check satisfactory to the University. Belmont University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
    },
    "https://belmont.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/10/home/requisition/4984?c=belmont": {
      "title": "Clinician Educator",
      "description": "Clinician Educator | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Clinician Educator United States | req4984 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search Clinician Educator We are now accepting applications for a qualified Clinician Educator at Belmont University. Employment Type Faculty Internal Job Category Tenure Track (Faculty) Full-Time Department College of Medicine Hours N/A Primary Duties and Responsibilities: The Department of Foundational and\nClinical Sciences (FCS) at the Thomas F. Frist, Jr. College of Medicine (FCoM)\nat Belmont University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-eligible Clinician\nEducator faculty position. The successful candidate will join a\ncollaborative, mission-driven faculty committed to developing and delivering an\ninnovative, integrated medical curriculum grounded in whole-person care and\ndedicated to the College’s vision of transforming health through compassion and\nservice. The Clinician Educator will contribute\nto the academic mission of the College of Medicine through teaching,\nscholarship, clinical expertise, and service. Candidates should possess\nexpertise in a clinical discipline relevant to undergraduate medical education,\nsuch as internal medicine, family medicine, pediatrics, surgery, emergency\nmedicine, neurology, psychiatry, or another medical specialty or subspecialty. The successful candidate will provide\nhigh-quality instruction across the medical curriculum, with particular\nemphasis on the pre-clerkship years, and work collaboratively with foundational\nscience and clinical faculty to integrate clinical medicine with the biomedical\nsciences. The Clinician Educator will play an important role in helping medical\nstudents apply foundational knowledge to clinical reasoning, diagnosis,\ntreatment, prevention, and patient-centered care. Clinician Educators are expected to\ncontribute to the development, delivery, and assessment of an integrated,\ncase-based, student-centered curriculum. Depending upon the candidate’s\nexpertise and assigned responsibilities, opportunities may also include participation\nin clinical skills education, simulation, case facilitation, clerkship\neducation, electives, and other clinical learning experiences. The\nposition also includes mentoring learners, engaging in scholarly activity,\nparticipating in curriculum development and assessment, and contributing to\ndepartmental and College initiatives, including those supporting continuous\nquality improvement and accreditation. Responsibilities: Teaching and\nCurriculum Development Deliver engaging, evidence-based clinical instruction through\n     lectures, small-group sessions, case-based learning, clinical skills\n     activities, simulation, and other instructional formats. Develop, revise, and maintain learning objectives, instructional\n     materials, clinical cases, and assessments aligned with course and program\n     outcomes and accreditation standards. Integrate clinical concepts across organ-system and\n     interdisciplinary courses and connect foundational biomedical science\n     principles with clinical presentation, diagnosis, treatment, prevention,\n     and patient care. Collaborate with foundational science and clinical faculty to\n     promote horizontal and vertical integration throughout the medical\n     curriculum. Utilize innovative and evidence-based teaching strategies, including\n     active learning, case-based learning, simulation, and technology-enhanced\n     instruction. Participate in the development and continuous improvement of\n     courses, assessments, clinical cases, and curricular content. Contribute to clinical skills education and other clinical learning\n     experiences consistent with the candidate’s expertise and assigned\n     responsibilities. Mentor and advise medical students in their academic, professional,\n     clinical, and scholarly development. Scholarship and\nResearch Maintain an active program of scholarship appropriate to the\n     candidate’s expertise and academic interests, which may include clinical,\n     translational, quality improvement, biomedical, or medical education\n     research. Disseminate scholarly work through peer-reviewed publications,\n     presentations, educational products, or other appropriate scholarly\n     venues. Pursue internal and external funding opportunities, when\n     appropriate, to support research, educational innovation, quality\n     improvement, or collaborative scholarly initiatives. Mentor and supervise medical students participating in research and\n     scholarly activities. Develop interdisciplinary collaborations within the College of\n     Medicine, Belmont University, affiliated clinical partners, and the\n     broader academic and healthcare community. Remain current with advances in clinical practice, medical\n     education, and areas of professional expertise and incorporate relevant\n     developments into teaching and scholarship. Service\nand Leadership Participate in departmental, College, and University committees and\n     contribute to shared governance and strategic initiatives. Contribute to accreditation activities, program evaluation, and\n     continuous quality improvement. Engage in professional service through relevant clinical,\n     scientific, educational, or professional organizations. Contribute to curriculum innovation, program development, and other\n     initiatives supporting the educational mission of the College. Participate in clinical activities, when appropriate to the faculty\n     member’s appointment, expertise, and departmental responsibilities. Support a collegial academic environment characterized by\n     collaboration, professionalism, respect, and commitment to the mission and\n     values of Belmont University and the College of Medicine. Appointment and\nEvaluation This is a full-time, 12-month,\ntenure-eligible faculty appointment in the Department of Foundational and\nClinical Sciences. Faculty rank and salary will be commensurate with\nqualifications and experience. Tenure-track faculty are evaluated on\nperformance in teaching, scholarship, and service consistent with the Belmont\nUniversity Faculty Handbook and College of Medicine criteria for academic\nadvancement. About the Thomas\nF. Frist, Jr. College of Medicine The Thomas F. Frist, Jr. College of\nMedicine at Belmont University in Nashville, Tennessee, is committed to\neducating physicians who embrace whole-person care, compassionate service, and\nlifelong learning. Founded on Belmont University’s Christ-centered mission, the\nCollege seeks faculty who value collaboration, academic excellence, service,\ninnovation, and the formation of physicians prepared to care for patients and\ncommunities with knowledge, compassion, and integrity. Required Qualifications : Belmont University seeks to attract and retain highly qualified faculty and staff that share the University's values and will contribute to its mission and vision to be a leader among teaching universities bringing together the best of liberal arts and professional education in a Christian community of learning and service. Demonstrated clinical expertise relevant to undergraduate medical\n     education. Evidence of effective teaching or demonstrated potential for\n     excellence in teaching medical students, residents, fellows, or other\n     health professions learners. Ability to integrate clinical medicine with foundational biomedical\n     sciences and apply foundational knowledge to clinical reasoning and\n     patient care. Commitment to innovative, learner-centered pedagogy and\n     interdisciplinary integration of foundational and clinical sciences. Commitment to scholarship and the mentorship of medical students in\n     scholarly activities. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and demonstrated\n     ability to work collaboratively within a multidisciplinary academic\n     environment. Preferred Experience teaching within a medical school or other health\n     professions education program. Experience in an integrated, organ-system-based, or case-based\n     medical curriculum. Experience teaching clinical reasoning, clinical skills, physical\n     diagnosis, simulation, or case-based learning. Ability to contribute meaningfully to instruction across multiple\n     areas of the medical curriculum. Record of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed\n     publications, conference presentations, clinical or educational research,\n     quality improvement, educational innovation, or other forms of scholarly\n     activity. Experience mentoring medical students in clinical, translational,\n     quality improvement, or medical education scholarship. Familiarity with active-learning methodologies, simulation,\n     educational technologies, and contemporary approaches to medical\n     education. Experience with curriculum design, assessment, program evaluation,\n     and accreditation processes, particularly within an LCME-accredited\n     medical education program. Experience in leadership within medical education, clinical\n     practice, or academic medicine. Required Education MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree from an accredited institution. Required Experience Board certification or board eligibility in a medical specialty or subspecialty relevant to the educational mission of the College. Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Tennessee, when clinical practice is included as part of the faculty member’s responsibilities. The selected candidate will be required to complete a background check satisfactory to the University. Belmont University participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Apply Now Share Save Job Belmont University is a Christ-centered community. The University faculty, administration, and staff uphold Jesus as the Christ and as the measure for all things. As a community seeking to uphold Christian standards of morality, ethics, and conduct, Belmont University holds high expectations of each person who chooses to join the community. In compliance with federal law, including provisions of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973/the Americans with Disabilities Act, Belmont University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, national or ethnic origin, age, disability, military service, or sexual orientation in its administration of education policies, programs or activities; its admissions policies; or employment. Consistent with applicable civil rights law, the University primarily hires employees of the Christian faith who are committed to support the mission of the University. The University has appointed the Chief Human Resources Officer/Deputy Title IX Coordinator to serve as coordinator of non-discrimination compliance for staff and faculty. The Director of Institutional Equity and Compliance and Title IX Coordinator serves as coordinator of compliance for Title IX (sex discrimination) issues and questions for students. Click here to learn more. *In accordance with accrediting standards for its professional education programs in law and medicine, the University does not apply religious criteria in hiring employees within those programs. All employees hired by the University, regardless of religious faith, shall be committed to Belmont’s Christ-centered, student-focused mission. Belmont's Office of Human Resources will conduct ongoing evaluations of the means by which candidates for employment are sought, to the end that the pool of candidates will include qualified women, minorities, veterans and disabled individuals. Belmont University is an equal opportunity employer under all applicable civil rights laws."
